.TH ANONIMO 1 "October 2026" "syslinuxos-anonimo 2026.10.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
anonimo \- route the TCP and DNS traffic of the computer through Tor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B anonimo start
.RB [ \-\-close\-apps | \-\-no\-close\-apps ]
.br
.B anonimo stop
.RB [ \-\-close\-apps | \-\-no\-close\-apps ]
.br
.B anonimo status
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.br
.B anonimo identity
.br
.B anonimo settings
.RB [ \-\-lan " on|off] [" \-\-bridges " none|obfs4|snowflake] [" \-\-boot " on|off] [" \-\-close\-apps " on|off]"
.br
.B anonimo bridges
.RB [ \-\-set " FILE|\-]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
When anonimo is on, every outgoing TCP connection and every DNS query of the
computer goes through Tor. All other outgoing traffic is blocked, except traffic
to the local network (when the direct local network option is on, as by default)
and DHCP.
anonimo adds its own nftables table (\fBinet anonimo\fR) and runs its own Tor
instance (\fBsyslinuxos\-anonimo\-tor.service\fR); it does not change ufw rules,
\fI/etc/resolv.conf\fR or \fI/etc/tor/torrc\fR. The graphical interface is
\fBsyslinuxos\-anonimo\fR.
Services of this computer (SSH, web server, file sharing) cannot answer hosts
outside the local network, nor local hosts when the direct local network option
is off. Connections to the Internet already open when anonimo switches on are
cut. With the
direct local network option on, local devices are reachable by IP address.
Starting, reloading or stopping \fBnftables.service\fR while anonimo is on
loads its table again; after loading nftables rules by hand
(\fBnft \-f\fR), run
.B anonimo start
again.
.SH EXIT STATUS
For \fBstatus\fR: 0 protected, 1 error, 2 connecting, 3 off.
Once protected, \fBstart\fR asks check.torproject.org whether traffic goes
through Tor and exits with 1 when it does not.
.SH FILES
.I /etc/syslinuxos-anonimo/settings.json
.br
.I /etc/syslinuxos-anonimo/obfs4-bridges
.SH RECOVERY
To get the normal network back, run
.BR "anonimo stop \-\-no\-close\-apps" ,
then
.BR "anonimo settings \-\-boot off" .
If anonimo does not respond, this command removes the table for the current
session only:
.BR "sudo nft delete table inet anonimo" .
With start at boot on, the table is loaded again at every boot until
"anonimo settings \-\-boot off" is run. With start at boot off, a reboot always
brings the normal network back.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tor (1),
.BR nft (8)
